 {:at "2026-09-09"
  :evidence ["evidence-all.log" "evidence-esp-uefi.log"]
  :profile :uefi-suite-union-plus-amd-vi-observation
  :core {:emitted 74 :declared 114 :pct 64.9}
  :route :b-a-defect-in-the-measurement-itself
  :note "The headline did not move and the measurement got better, which is the
         only combination this ledger should ever be pleased about.
         Booting the guest behind AMD-Vi for the first time -- the K16's actual
         IOMMU class, which QEMU 10.0.3 can emulate -- showed
         AIUEOS_DMA_POLICY_OK emitted as `dmar=absent test-only-unisolated`.
         Under VT-d the same marker reads `dmar=validated dma=vtd-isolated`.
         One name, two opposite meanings, and this script counted names: it was
         crediting an explicitly unisolated boot with isolation.
         Fixed by letting the taxonomy require a payload substring for markers
         whose _OK is a report rather than a verdict. The first substring chosen
         was `isolated` and it discriminated NOTHING, because
         `test-only-unisolated` contains it -- the negative case is spelled as
         the positive with a prefix. Caught by running the control rather than
         trusting the string; `validated` separates them.
         Verified both directions: AMD-Vi-only evidence now reads isolation 3/6
         with DMA_POLICY unproven, and evidence containing a real VT-d boot
         still reads 6/6. The union stays 74/114 because it genuinely contains
         an isolated boot."}
 {:at "2026-09-09"
  :evidence ["evidence-all.log" "evidence-esp-uefi.log"]
  :profile :uefi-suite-union
  :core {:emitted 74 :declared 115 :pct 64.3}
  :route :b-a-gap-closed-halfway-and-the-denominator-grew
  :note "64.9 to 64.3, and down is right. acpi.c now discovers and validates
         IVRS -- AMD's counterpart to DMAR, and the table that actually exists
         on this board -- with the same fail-closed shape as DMAR: a table that
         claims to be IVRS and does not validate stops the boot rather than
         being skipped, because skipping leaves the present flag at 0, which is
         indistinguishable from a machine with no IOMMU, and that is exactly
         the state the DMA policy reads as permission to run unisolated.
         The guest now reports three distinguishable states instead of one:
         IVRS_OK validated, IVRS_FAIL described-but-refused, IVRS_ABSENT
         no-amd-iommu-described.
         AIUEOS_IVRS joins the isolation row, so :declared grows to 115 and the
         headline falls. That is the honest direction and the reason :declared
         is recorded beside :emitted: the tree grew a capability it has not
         proven.
         And a correction to this session's own claim. I wrote that
         amd-ivrs-isolation was NOT hardware-gated because QEMU 10.0.3 offers
         amd-iommu. QEMU has the DEVICE and does not hand the guest an IVRS
         TABLE: attached, with and without intremap=on, the guest reports
         IVRS_ABSENT. The parser is correct by construction and untested
         against a real table. Marked as such rather than counted."}
